1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:06,318
A thin, bent piece of silver comes out of the soil, stamped with small symbols.

2
00:00:06,318 --> 00:00:16,480
It is a punch-marked coin, one of the oldest coins in India. Money means trade, and trade means towns. Why do coins appear now?

3
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:38,289
How do we know? Archaeologists dug up the fine black pottery and walled towns of the period.

4
00:00:38,289 --> 00:00:45,591
Buddhist and Jain texts list the sixteen states. Punch-marked coins show that trade was growing.

5
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:08,700
After about six hundred BCE, the Ganga plains saw a second wave of cities. Iron tools, new farmland and trade made them possible.

6
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:12,731
This is called the second urbanisation, after the Harappan cities.

7
00:01:12,731 --> 00:01:18,225
Early Buddhist and Jain texts list sixteen great states, the Mahajanapadas.

8
00:01:18,225 --> 00:01:33,577
They include Anga, Magadha, Kashi, Kosala, Vajji, Malla, Chedi, Vatsa, Kuru, Panchala, Matsya, Surasena, Assaka, Avanti, Gandhara and Kamboja.

9
00:01:33,577 --> 00:01:41,280
Most were kingdoms. But Vajji and Malla were ruled by assemblies, called gana-sanghas, a form of republic.

10
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:51,442
Four states stood out: Magadha, Kosala, Vatsa and Avanti. In time Magadha won. Magadha was strong for good reasons.

11
00:01:51,442 --> 00:01:58,034
It had iron ore nearby, fertile land, and rivers for trade. Its forests gave it war elephants.

12
00:01:58,034 --> 00:02:04,278
Its early capital was Rajagriha, and later Pataliputra. New goods marked this age.

13
00:02:04,278 --> 00:02:11,329
Coins were struck, and a shiny black pottery, called Northern Black Polished Ware, spread across the plains.

14
00:02:25,417 --> 00:02:33,223
Old name, new name. The capital of Magadha, Pataliputra, stood beside the Ganga and the Son.

15
00:02:33,223 --> 00:02:38,502
Its ruins lie under the modern city of Patna, the capital of Bihar.

16
00:02:52,583 --> 00:03:02,736
Carve this tip into memory. Sixteen states, two republics. Magadha wins with iron and rivers. Sixteen states, then one winner.

17
00:03:02,736 --> 00:03:07,012
Remember iron, rivers and elephants as the reasons.

18
00:03:21,083 --> 00:03:37,071
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name this early silver coin, stamped with symbols. The answer: a punch-marked coin.

19
00:03:37,071 --> 00:03:49,148
Number two. Which Mahajanapada became the most powerful? The answer: Magadha. Number three.

20
00:03:49,148 --> 00:04:01,903
Name this shiny black pottery of the age. The answer: Northern Black Polished Ware. Now one line for Mains.

21
00:04:01,903 --> 00:04:06,579
Why did Magadha emerge as the most powerful Mahajanapada?

22
00:04:24,650 --> 00:04:34,637
The coin goes into its case. Money was now moving between towns. Three facts to take away. Silver, stamped with small symbols.

23
00:04:34,637 --> 00:04:43,785
Used from about the sixth century BCE. A sign of growing trade and towns. Next gallery: The Jain Path.

